# Exo Free — findings by Claude Opus 5

- Source: unattributed / anonymous lab (`exo-free`, OpenCode Zen route)
- Date: 2026-10-08 (UTC)
- Overview and scoring methodology: `../../model-comparison.md`
- Cross-model signed log: `../../model-findings.md`

## EXCLUDED — Overall Score cannot be computed

**This file is deliberately suffixed `.md.excluded`.** Per `RULES.md`, that suffix applies when an Overall Score cannot be computed because there is zero verified data for the exact model/ID. That is the case here, and the reason is simply timing plus anonymity: **`exo-free` was dropped anonymously on OpenCode Zen on 2026-10-06, two days before this research date, by an unnamed lab, and no benchmark score of any kind has been published for it.** The model is real and usable — unlike `grok-5` — and it has a distinct identity of its own — unlike `owl-alpha` — but there is nothing quantitative to score.

## Model card

- **Name:** Exo Free
- **Short description:** An **anonymous free route on OpenCode Zen**, slipped into the model picker without announcement. The community summary is accurate: "OpenCode Zen just slipped another stealth model into the picker: Exo Free. ~1M context, reasoning on, $0. **Lab unknown.** Free while they collect feedback." Specs are unusually generous for a free route — **1M context, 131K output, reasoning, vision and tool calling at zero cost** — which is precisely why it attracted attention.
- **Provider / access:** **OpenCode Zen**, confirmed registered under the OpenCode namespace. [Pi](https://pi.dev/models/opencode/exo-free) records: Model `exo-free`, Provider `opencode`, API `openai-completions`, Base URL `https://opencode.ai/zen/v1`. Querying Zen's public `https://opencode.ai/zen/v1/models` endpoint confirms it is "officially registered under the OpenCode namespace and actively deployed" ([Free AI API](https://freeaiapi.org/articles/opencode-exo-free-guide)).
- **Release / knowledge:** **2026-10-06, approximately 22:30 UTC.** ArtificialWatch's automated sweep first observed it in models.dev at **2026-10-06 22:54 UTC**; it went viral on X the following afternoon. Knowledge cutoff: not disclosed. **This model is two days old as of this report** — the absence of benchmark coverage is a function of age as much as anonymity.
- **IDs:** `exo-free` / `opencode/exo-free` (OpenCode Zen). **Free: $0.00 input, $0.00 output, $0.00 cached reads**, "available for a limited time, with no end date stated" ([Free AI Credits](https://freetokens.custats.info/offers/opencode-exo-free.html)).
- **Context window:** **1,048,576 tokens (1M)** with **131,072 max output** — convergent across Pi, models.dev, TypingMind's pricing calculator and Free AI API.
- **Modalities:** **Text + image in → text out.** Capabilities listed as "attachments, reasoning, tool calling, image input". Reasoning: **yes**, though one community report specifies "**reasoning only on high**" — i.e. effort may not be selectable on this route.
- **Pricing:** **$0.00 across the board**, with an explicit and important trade: "During the free period, **collected data may be used to improve the model; avoid sending sensitive or confidential material**." Free AI API is blunter: "The endpoint offers **no zero-data-retention guarantees. Prompts and uploaded screenshots will be used for future model training. Never input proprietary source code, production keys, or sensitive customer records.**"
- **Operational reality worth recording:** "Surging traffic has triggered frequent **`Endpoint is unavailable` notices and 429 rate limits**." This is not a stable route.
- **Architecture:** **Undisclosed.** No vendor, no parameter count, no training details.

### Raw benchmarks found

**None.** Not sparse — zero. A direct fetch of `benchlm.ai/md/models/exo-free.md` returns **404**, and there is no BenchmarkList, Artificial Analysis or Vals AI page, which is expected for a two-day-old anonymous route.

Every category reads the same: Agent / tool use, reasoning, coding, multimodal and long context — **no verified public score found.**

**What does exist, and why none of it is scorable:**

**1. An identity fingerprint, not a capability measurement.** Testing with the third-party tool `lm.ikale.io` "matched its generation patterns to Anthropic's flagship model **Claude Opus 5.5 with an overwhelming 99.4% confidence score**", with the runner-up claude-fable-5.1 at just 0.4%. The method is "prompting the model for approximately 300 pseudo-random numbers within a set interval, paired with tokenizer probes to inspect physical token boundaries."

**I am not scoring this, and the publishing source explains better than I could why nobody should.** Free AI API's own critique: "The primary limitation is **closed-set matching**. As the tool notes, confidence reflects **relative** probability among known reference models. **If exo-free is an uncataloged model heavily trained on Opus 5.5 synthetic outputs, the algorithm will naturally assign maximum probability to the closest candidate in its bank.**" The same article lays out three mutually exclusive theories and endorses none: **(A)** a confidential blind evaluation by an upstream lab under NDA, **(B)** a routing misconfiguration temporarily directing `exo-free` traffic to a paid Opus pool, or **(C)** a distilled open-weights model fine-tuned on Opus 5.5 chain-of-thought traces. A separate X claim contradicts all three: "I cracked the Exo Free stealth model: underneath **it's not a model, but a router with Claude and GPT inside.**"

Those claims cannot all be true, and 99.4% confidence on a closed-set numeric-bias probe is not evidence about coding ability, retrieval quality or agentic reliability regardless of which one is.

**2. Qualitative community stress tests, reported as pass/fail with no scores:**

- **"Pelican on a Bicycle" spatial test** — write Python to render the scene in Blender or Three.js. Reported outcome: "successfully accounted for mechanics like feet on pedals, wings on handlebars, and ground contact **without clipping or spatial distortion**."
- **Convenience-store Blender modelling** — generate a complete Blender Python script for a store with aisles, stock, refrigerators, counters and lighting. Reported outcome: "**executed cleanly on the first attempt**, producing structured depth and shading."
- **Multimodal diagnosis** — feeding "complex microservice architecture diagrams or mobile error screenshots yielded accurate diagnostic tracing and actionable refactoring advice."

These are credible-sounding and genuinely useful signals that the model is capable. They are also **single-tester, single-attempt, unscored anecdotes with no published prompts, no pass rate, no comparison baseline and no reproduction**. There is no defensible way to convert "rendered a pelican without clipping" into a number on a 1–100 coding scale.

**3. Circumstantial inference from the specs.** A 1M context with 131K output, vision and reasoning offered free suggests a substantial model behind the route. It does not measure one.

### Why exclusion rather than low evidence-absence scores

I have been deliberately inconsistent-looking across this queue, so the standard is worth stating. I **scored** `omen-alpha` at 34.8 despite near-total opacity, because one dated, methodology-described number existed that belonged to that identifier and nothing else — an OpenCode coding score of 23.14/40 across four projects. I **excluded** `owl-alpha` because its only numbers belonged to `longcat-2.0`, which has its own folder. I **excluded** `grok-5` because the model does not exist.

`exo-free` fails on a fourth distinct ground: **the model exists, is usable, and has its own identity, but literally no capability number has been produced for it yet.** The only figure anywhere near it — 99.4% — measures *which other model it statistically resembles*, and its own publisher explains that the metric cannot distinguish "is Opus 5.5" from "was distilled from Opus 5.5" from "routes to Opus 5.5 sometimes".

The tempting move is to treat the fingerprint as a licence to import Claude Opus 5.5's benchmark suite. That would be wrong three times over: the attribution is unverified by the source that produced it, a competing credible claim says the backend is a multi-model router, and a fourth possibility — Theory B, a transient routing misconfiguration — would mean today's behaviour is not even tomorrow's behaviour on the same endpoint. Attributing a frontier model's scores to an anonymous free route on that basis would put a fabricated frontier-tier Overall into this dataset.

**Reversal criteria, stated explicitly:** this exclusion should be lifted as soon as **any** of the following appears — a benchmark result published against the `exo-free` endpoint by any evaluator (BenchLM, BenchmarkList, Artificial Analysis, Vals AI or a reproducible community harness); an OpenCode or upstream-lab announcement attributing the route to a named model; or the route's graduation to a named, priced model with a card. Given that the model is **two days old**, the first of these is likely within weeks, and this folder should then be re-researched from scratch with a fresh `.md` rather than this file being edited.

### Normalized scores (1–100)

Not computed. All five quality dimensions have **zero verified capability data**, and the cost dimension — while trivially excellent at $0.00 — cannot carry an Overall on its own, nor would a cost-only score be meaningful for a route with no zero-data-retention guarantee and documented 429 rate limiting. Assigning numbers here would mean either converting single-attempt Blender anecdotes into benchmark scores or importing Claude Opus 5.5's results on the strength of a closed-set fingerprint that its own publisher says cannot support that inference. Per `RULES.md`, this is the condition under which the `.md.excluded` suffix applies, and no parseable score lines are written in this file.

